# `dist/` is committed (`git add -f` against the `dist/` line in `.gitignore`):
# `bin.maximal` is `dist/main.js` and the `exports` map points at `dist/lib`, so
# a git-dependency install RUNS these exact bytes with no build step. See
# docs/bun-version-policy.md and scripts/ops/check-bindings.ts.
#
# `-merge` — DO NOT auto-merge these. A bundle is a text file with thousands of
# lines, so git's ort strategy will happily 3-way merge two independently-built
# bundles whose hunks do not overlap, and produce a `dist/main.js` that
# corresponds to NO source tree at all. Measured: `gh pr update-branch` on
# maximal-core#116 — a PR that changes no `src/` file — merged a bundle built by
# one Bun version with one built by another and reported "Auto-merging
# dist/main.js", silently. `bindings:check` then failed on a branch whose diff
# contained nothing that could explain it.
#
# With this attribute git refuses and raises a conflict instead, leaving OURS in
# the work tree unmodified (no conflict markers spliced into a 7 MB bundle,
# which would make it unrunnable). The resolution is never a hand-edit: rebuild.
#
#     bun run container:run -- bun run build && git add -f dist/main.js
#
# `dist/lib/*.d.ts` has the identical exposure — verified the same way — so the
# rule covers the whole directory rather than just the bundle.
dist/** -merge
